<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-mixed-strategies" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mixed Strategies and the Fundamental Theorem</title>

  <p>
    Some games don't have any Nash equilibria of the kind we've seen so far.
    In this chapter we let players randomize, and it turns out that fixes the
    problem.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-1-setup">
    <title>Section 5.1, Mixed Strategies and the Definition</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.1 from the beginning up to Theorem 5.1 (Nash's
      Existence Theorem).</alert> Stop right before it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Take a minute to check the book's claim that Tennis has no Nash
      equilibrium using best response. After that, most of the reading is
      notation. It looks like a lot, but we'll unpack it below using Tennis.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's the Tennis payoff matrix with each player's probabilities written
    in the margins. This is how the book draws it in Section 5.2.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-tennis-pq">
    <title>Tennis, with mixed strategies</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1-q</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Receiver: <m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>Receiver: <m>f</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell>Server: <m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>(.4, .6)</cell>
        <cell>(.8, .2)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1-p</m></cell>
        <cell>Server: <m>f</m></cell>
        <cell>(.7, .3)</cell>
        <cell>(.1, .9)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    So the server's mixed strategy is <m>\sigma_1 = pb + (1-p)f</m> and the
    receiver's is <m>\sigma_2 = qb + (1-q)f</m>. The book's convention is that
    <m>p</m>'s always belong to Player 1 and <m>q</m>'s to Player 2, and we'll
    stick with that all chapter. (Heads up: when you get to Section 5.2, the
    sentence right before this table has <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> swapped. The
    table and the equations after it are correct.)
  </p>

  <p>
    A few things about the notation:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>.6b + .4f</m> isn't arithmetic. You can't add strategies! It's a
          recipe: serve to the backhand 60% of the time and the forehand 40%
          of the time.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>p_{s_i}</m> is the probability Player <m>i</m> puts on her
          strategy <m>s_i</m>. The book writes it this way to avoid double
          subscripts.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          A strategy is <term>active</term> if its probability is more than
          <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          A pure strategy is just a mixed strategy with probability <m>1</m> on
          one strategy. So everything from Chapter 3 is still here.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\sigma_{-i}</m> and <m>(\tau_i, \sigma_{-i})</m> mean the same
          thing as in Chapter 3, just with mixed strategies.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    Now let's break down equation (5.2). In Tennis, the server's expected
    payoff adds up all four cells, each weighted by how likely we are to land
    in it:
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) =
      \underbrace{pq}_{\substack{\text{prob. server goes } b\\ \text{and receiver expects } b}}
      \cdot
      \underbrace{(.4)}_{\substack{\text{server's payoff}\\ \text{in that cell}}}
      + p(1-q)(.8) + (1-p)q(.7) + (1-p)(1-q)(.1)
    </me>
    The general version says the same thing:
    <me>
      \pi_i(\sigma_1, \ldots, \sigma_n) = \sum_{\text{all } (s_1, \ldots, s_n)}
      \underbrace{p_{s_1} p_{s_2} \cdots p_{s_n}}_{\substack{\text{prob. that } s_1, \ldots, s_n\\ \text{all happen at once}}}
      \;
      \underbrace{\pi_i(s_1, \ldots, s_n)}_{\substack{\text{Player } i\text{'s payoff}\\ \text{when they do}}}
    </me>
    We get to multiply the probabilities because the players choose
    independently.
  </p>

  <p>
    Equation (5.6) is the same sum, just grouped by Player <m>i</m>'s
    strategies. In Tennis, group the four cells by the server's row:
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) = p \cdot \pi_1(b, \sigma_2) + (1-p) \cdot \pi_1(f, \sigma_2)
    </me>
    Here <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m> is what the server gets if she always serves
    to the backhand while the receiver keeps mixing. The general version:
    <me>
      \pi_i(\sigma) = \sum_{\text{all } s_i \in S_i}
      \underbrace{p_{s_i}}_{\substack{\text{how often}\\ i \text{ plays } s_i}}
      \;
      \underbrace{\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i})}_{\substack{\text{what } s_i \text{ earns her against}\\ \text{everyone else's mixing}}}
    </me>
    So a player's payoff from a mixed strategy is a weighted average of what
    her pure strategies earn. Keep this in mind, it's the key to everything
    on this page!
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, the definition. It's the Chapter 3 definition with <m>\sigma</m>
    in place of <m>s</m>. A mixed strategy profile <m>\sigma^*</m> is a Nash
    equilibrium if for each player <m>i</m>,
    <me>
      \underbrace{\pi_i(\sigma^*)}_{\substack{i\text{'s payoff when}\\ \text{everyone plays their part}}}
      \;\geq\;
      \underbrace{\pi_i(\sigma_i, \sigma^*_{-i})}_{\substack{i\text{'s payoff if she alone}\\ \text{switches to another mix}}}
      \quad \text{for every mixed strategy } \sigma_i.
    </me>
    One catch: there are now infinitely many <m>\sigma_i</m> to check, since
    every possible mix counts. We'll see how to get around that.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's test a specific profile in Tennis: <m>\sigma_1 = .6b + .4f</m> and
    <m>\sigma_2 = .7b + .3f</m>. Section 5.2 will show where these numbers come
    from. For now, just take them as given.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-tennis-pi1-b">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The receiver uses <m>\sigma_2 = .7b + .3f</m>. Compute
        <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m>, the server's expected payoff if she always
        serves to the backhand.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>.52</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Read across the server's <m>b</m> row using her payoffs,
            weighted by the receiver's probabilities:
            <m>.4(.7) + .8(.3) = .52</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.56</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            You weighted by <m>.6</m> and <m>.4</m>, which are the server's own
            probabilities. If she always serves to the backhand, what's left to
            average over is what the receiver does, so use <m>.7</m> and
            <m>.3</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.49</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            You read down the <m>b</m> column. The server's strategies are the
            rows, so <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m> lives in the <m>b</m> row.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.48</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            You used the second number in each cell, which is the receiver's
            payoff. <m>\pi_1</m> is the server's payoff, so use the first
            numbers.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The same computation on the <m>f</m> row gives
    <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = .7(.7) + .1(.3) = .52</m>. The same number!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-tennis-any-p">
    <statement>
      <p>
        We know <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2) = .52</m> and
        <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = .52</m>. For any <m>p</m>, what is
        <m>\pi_1(pb + (1-p)f, \sigma_2)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>.52</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. By (5.6) it's <m>p(.52) + (1-p)(.52) = .52</m>. Averaging
            two equal numbers gives that number back, no matter the weights.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.52p</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That only counts the serves to the backhand. The other
            <m>1-p</m> of the time she serves to the forehand, which also
            earns <m>.52</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>1.04</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            She can't collect both. Each serve goes one way or the other, so
            her payoff is a weighted average, not a sum.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>.52</m> when <m>p = .6</m>, and less for other values of <m>p</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Tempting, but try <m>p = 0</m> or <m>p = 1</m>. She still gets
            <m>.52</m>. Against <m>q = .7</m>, her choice of <m>p</m> doesn't
            change her payoff at all.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So no mix does better for the server. The receiver's side works the same
    way. Against <m>p = .6</m>, both of her pure strategies earn her
    <m>.48</m> (check <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, b) = .6(.6) + .3(.4)</m> if you want),
    so no mix does better for her either. That means
    <m>(.6b + .4f,\ .7b + .3f)</m> is a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium!
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice what <m>p = .6</m> actually does. It doesn't matter to the server,
    since every <m>p</m> gives her <m>.52</m>. What it does is make the
    <em>receiver's</em> two numbers equal. Likewise <m>q = .7</m> makes the
    server's two numbers equal. Each player's mix keeps the other one
    indifferent, and Section 5.2 uses exactly this to find <m>p</m> and
    <m>q</m>.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-1-theorems">
    <title>Section 5.1, Nash's Theorem and the Fundamental Theorem</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read from Theorem 5.1 through the paragraph right after Theorem
      5.2, the one beginning <q>This theorem just says.</q></alert> Skip the
      proof, and stop at the paragraph beginning <q>In the remainder of this
      section.</q>
    </p>
    <p>
      The paragraph after Theorem 5.1 connects back to Section 4.1: mixed
      equilibria assume players care about expected payoff. The paragraph
      after Theorem 5.2 is the whole theorem in plain words, so read it
      carefully.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Condition (1) of Theorem 5.2 is exactly what we just found in Tennis: the
    server's active strategies both earn <m>.52</m>, and the receiver's both
    earn <m>.48</m>. Condition (2) is about inactive strategies, and there
    aren't any in the Tennis equilibrium since both players use both
    strategies. So there's nothing to check. To see condition (2) at work, we
    need a profile where someone leaves a strategy unused.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-condition-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the server always serves to the forehand (<m>p = 0</m>) and
        the receiver uses <m>q = .5</m>. Then <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2) = .6</m>
        and <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = .4</m>. Is this a Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. Her only active strategy is <m>f</m>, but her inactive
            strategy <m>b</m> earns more. Condition (2) fails.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. She'd do better by switching to <m>b</m>. Condition (2)
            is the Chapter 3 check: can someone gain by switching to a
            strategy she isn't using?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. She only uses one strategy, so there's nothing to compare.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Condition (2) compares her active strategies to the ones she
            <em>isn't</em> using. Here <m>b</m> is unused and earns more, so
            she wants to switch.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. Condition (1) fails, because <m>.6 \neq .4</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right answer, wrong reason. Condition (1) only compares active
            strategies with each other, and <m>b</m> isn't active. The
            comparison between an active and an inactive strategy is
            condition (2).
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's reason out the next three theorems before reading them.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-tennis-equilibrium-payoff">
    <title>The server's payoff at the Tennis equilibrium</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        At the equilibrium <m>(.6b + .4f,\ .7b + .3f)</m>, what is the
        server's expected payoff?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>The long way.</alert> Use (5.2) on all four cells:
        <me>
          .42(.4) + .18(.8) + .28(.7) + .12(.1) = .168 + .144 + .196 + .012 = .52
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>The short way.</alert> Her strategy <m>b</m> is active and
        earns <m>.52</m>, and so does <m>f</m>. Her payoff is a weighted
        average of the two, so it's <m>.52</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    That's <alert>Theorem 5.3</alert>. At a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,
    all of a player's active strategies earn the same amount <m>K</m>. Her
    equilibrium payoff is <m>K</m>, and so is any mix of her active
    strategies. You already saw the second part in the <m>.52</m> checkpoint!
  </p>

  <p>
    Next, pure strategies are special mixed strategies, so the new definition
    should agree with the Chapter 3 one on pure profiles. One direction is
    quick. Suppose a pure profile <m>s^*</m> is a mixed strategy Nash
    equilibrium. Then no player can gain by switching to <em>any</em> mixed
    strategy. That includes the pure ones, so no player can gain by switching
    to a pure strategy either. That's the Chapter 3 definition. What about the
    other direction?
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-pure-vs-mixed-deviation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(s_1^*, s_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium in the Chapter 3
        sense, so Player 1 can't gain by switching to any pure strategy. Could
        she gain by switching to some mixed strategy <m>\tau_1</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. By (5.6), <m>\tau_1</m>'s payoff is a weighted average of her
            pure strategies' payoffs, and none of those beat her equilibrium
            payoff.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If every number you're averaging is at most
            <m>\pi_1(s^*)</m>, the average is too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. A mix can combine the strengths of several pure strategies.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Against a fixed opponent, a mix is just a weighted average of its
            pure strategies' payoffs. An average can never beat the largest
            thing you're averaging.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            You'd have to check every possible mix to know.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's exactly the work (5.6) saves you. Every mix is a weighted
            average of pure strategy payoffs, so checking the pure strategies
            is enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    That's <alert>Theorem 5.4</alert>. For pure strategy profiles, the new
    definition agrees with the old one. Every pure Nash equilibrium from
    Chapter 3 is still an equilibrium, and allowing mixed strategies can only
    add new ones.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-tennis-not-strict">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall that a Nash equilibrium is <term>strict</term> if every player
        does strictly worse by switching. Is the Tennis equilibrium strict?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. The server can switch to always serving to the backhand and
            still get <m>.52</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. By Theorem 5.3, any mix of her active strategies earns
            the same, so she has plenty of switches that tie.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. No player can do better by switching.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's just the definition of a Nash equilibrium. Strict means
            every switch makes you <em>worse</em>, and here some switches
            leave the server exactly where she was.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes, since it's the only Nash equilibrium of the game.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Being the only equilibrium and being strict are different things.
            Strictness is about whether switching can ever tie.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    That's <alert>Theorem 5.5</alert>. Only pure equilibria can be strict.
    Whenever a player mixes, Theorem 5.3 hands her other strategies that tie,
    so some switch always does just as well.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-1-rest">
    <title>Section 5.1, The Rest</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 5.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      We just reasoned through Theorems 5.3, 5.4, and 5.5, so this should go
      quickly. The last two paragraphs are new:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In most two-player games, both players use the same number of
            active strategies. Section 5.2 uses this to organize its search.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Eliminating dominated strategies still works with mixed strategies.
            We'll use this in Poker.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
